  "content": "# Binary Search and `lower_bound`\n\n## The Big Idea: Binary Search\n\nImagine you're looking up a name in a **physical phone book**. You wouldn't start at page 1 and scan every single entry. Instead, you'd open the book roughly in the middle, see if the name you want comes before or after the current page, and then **throw away half the book**. You repeat this, halving the search space each time, until you find your name.\n\nThat's binary search: **repeatedly halving the search space** by comparing your target to the middle element.\n\n> \u26a0\ufe0f **Critical requirement:** Binary search only works on a **sorted** list. If the data isn't sorted, the \"throw away half\" logic makes no sense.\n\n### Why is it so fast?\n\nEach step cuts the remaining candidates in half:\n\n```\n1,000 items  \u2192  ~10 steps   (because 2\u00b9\u2070 = 1,024)\n1,000,000    \u2192  ~20 steps\n1,000,000,000 \u2192  ~30 steps\n```\n\nThis is why we",
